{
  "dom_id": "step-q61360",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/66d5a573ea6ca96486c172021b8948273ac593a80b9eae335681a20b1dc1bb0b.png)\n\nThe model above represents $3$ wholes. Use the model to determine the missing number in the following division problem.\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\boxed{\\,\\,}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split each whole into fourths.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/9f36fbcc4441247dc12da5e005d98f750df47fdeb3f080b3eb04f54d9dc37e7d.png)\n\n \n\n- Next, we create groups of $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/f0994c24f8452627e3ed898ed23f0da9ba306176ff9d73d5bfe03eb6434e9abf.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, we count the groups.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/5e236ffc51b1faeb2f38df7fc022bd1b28a0e7dea093310941b251b24601f587.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $3$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{3}{4},$ we get $4$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\boxed{\\text{4}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the missing number is $4.$",
